12}, {"species": "starlet", "min": 95, "max": 97, "weight": 10, "time": ["dawn", "day", "dusk"]}, {"species": "pinchkin", "min": 95, "max": 97, "weight": 12}, {"species": "duneskink", "min": 95, "max": 97, "weight": 10}, {"species": "swiftlet", "min": 95, "max": 97, "weight": 8, "weather": ["gale", "clear"]}, {"species": "petrelash", "min": 95, "max": 97, "weight": 8, "weather": ["gale", "downpour", "drizzle"]}, {"species": "wideling", "min": 95, "max": 97, "weight": 8}, {"species": "dolphlet", "min": 95, "max": 97, "weight": 6, "weather": ["sunshine", "clear"]}], "water": [{"species": "dolphlet", "min": 95, "max": 97, "weight": 30}, {"species": "swelklet", "min": 95, "max": 97, "weight": 22}, {"species": "plungebeak", "min": 95, "max": 97, "weight": 14}, {"species": "pinchkin", "min": 95, "max": 97, "weight": 14}, {"species": "seaventure", "min": 96, "max": 98, "weight": 4}, {"species": "gannetling", "min": 95, "max": 97, "weight": 16}]}, "signs": [{"x": 47, "y": 29, "text": "HAMILTON, BERMUDA. A city in the sense that it has a cathedral. Pedestrians keep left, scooters keep everything else."}, {"x": 34, "y": 23, "text": "GIBBS HILL, to the south-west. Climb it. Bring a flask, a camera and your sense of humour about the stairs."}, {"x": 14, "y": 15, "text": "THE ROYAL NAVAL DOCKYARD. Cannon, cruise ships and a very good craft market."}, {"x": 64, "y": 25, "text": "CRYSTAL CAVES. Cave tours every half hour. The stalactites are not for licking."}, {"x": 70, "y": 39, "text": "NONSUCH ISLAND. Nature reserve. Cahow burrows: look, do not touch. The bar is only walkable at low tide. The cahows consider this a feature."}, {"x": 11, "y": 18, "text": "PLAQUE: The English flagship Sea Venture, bound for Jamestown in Virginia, struck Bermuda's reefs in a hurricane in 1609. Everyone aboard got ashore. They spent ten months building two small ships from local cedar and sailed on in 1610, and some stayed. The wreck is why Bermuda is English, and possibly why Shakespeare wrote The Tempest.", "plaque": "bermuda_sea_venture", "title": "The Sea Venture"}, {"x": 21, "y": 24, "text": "PLAQUE: Admiral Sir George Somers captained the Sea Venture. He sailed on to Virginia, came back for supplies, and died here in 1610. The islands were long called the Somers Isles, and Bermuda's coat of arms still shows the wreck of his ship, held by a lion.", "plaque": "bermuda_somers", "title": "Admiral Somers"}, {"x": 12, "y": 16, "text": "PLAQUE: After the American colonies were lost, the Royal Navy needed a new base in the western Atlantic. Building began on Ireland Island in 1809 and went on for decades, much of it by convicts sent out from Britain. Today the old stores hold a museum, a craft market and a berth for cruise ships.", "plaque": "bermuda_dockyard", "title": "The Royal Naval Dockyard"}, {"x": 17, "y": 13, "text": "PLAQUE: Built between 1823 and 1831 for the Dockyard's senior officer, from local limestone with cast iron brought out from England. It looks across the Great Sound and now houses the National Museum of Bermuda.", "plaque": "bermuda_commissioner", "title": "Commissioner's House"}, {"x": 32, "y": 28, "text": "PLAQUE: Lit in 1846, with a cast-iron tower shipped out in pieces. Its lamp sits about 110 metres above the sea. Bermuda's reefs had been eating ships for two hundred years, and this was built to make them stop.", "plaque": "bermuda_gibbs_hill", "title": "Gibbs Hill Lighthouse"}, {"x": 63, "y": 29, "text": "PLAQUE: The Bermuda petrel, or cahow, was hunted by early settlers and their pigs until it was thought extinct for around three hundred years. In 1951 a few pairs were found nesting on rocky islets. Today they breed in artificial burrows on Nonsuch Island, with a warden counting every egg.", "plaque": "bermuda_cahow", "title": "The Cahow"}, {"x": 38, "y": 29, "text": "PLAQUE: Bermuda's pink beaches get their blush from the shells of tiny red-and-white sea creatures called foraminifera, ground up with the coral and shell sand. Yes, you may take a photo. No, you may not take the beach.", "plaque": "bermuda_pink_sand", "title": "Pink Sand"}, {"x": 49, "y": 30, "text": "PLAQUE: A circular stone arch, copied from Chinese garden gateways. Bermudian moongates are said to bring luck to couples who step through them, which is why there is nearly always a wedding photographer standing by.", "plaque": "bermuda_moongate", "title": "The Moongate"}, {"x": 41, "y": 27, "text": "PLAQUE: Bermuda's masked and feathered Gombey troupes dance to drum and whistle at Christmas, Boxing Day and New Year. The tradition blends West African, Caribbean, Native American and British mumming roots, and troupes pass costumes and steps down the generations.", "plaque": "bermuda_gombey", "title": "Gombey Dancers"}, {"x": 20, "y": 17, "text": "PLAQUE: Every summer, St George's and Somerset play a two-day cricket match, and the whole island takes the days off as Emancipation Day and Somers' Day. It has been played since the early 1900s and is part cricket, part carnival, part family reunion.", "plaque": "bermuda_cup_match", "title": "Cup Match"}, {"x": 48, "y": 19, "text": "PLAQUE: Bermuda's Parliament first met in 1620, which makes it one of the oldest in the Commonwealth. It sits in the Sessions House in Hamilton, and the Governor is appointed by the Crown.", "plaque": "bermuda_sessions", "title": "The Parliament"}, {"x": 9, "y": 22, "text": "PLAQUE: The first settlers built houses and ships from Bermuda cedar, which is really a juniper. A scale-insect blight in the 1940s killed most of the trees, and replanting goes on today. Cedar furniture is still a very Bermudian thing to inherit.", "plaque": "bermuda_cedar", "title": "Bermuda Cedar"}, {"x": 36, "y": 17, "text": "PLAQUE: Bermuda had a railway from 1931 to 1948, nicknamed the Rattle and Shake. Private cars were banned on the island until 1946, so for a while the train was the only grown-up way to get about. The old track is now a walking trail.", "plaque": "bermuda_railway", "title": "The Rattle and Shake"}], "legend_extra": {"_water": {"deep": "#0b4f74", "deep_top": "#2aa3b5", "shallow": "#3cc7c0"}, "$": {"tile": "sea", "colour": "#17708c"}, "~": {"tile": "water", "colour": "#2c9db0"}, "w": {"tile": "shallow", "colour": "#5fd3c6"}, ".": {"tile": "grass", "colour": "#6aa63c"}, ",": {"tile": "tall_grass", "colour": "#58963a", "prop": "short"}, "s": {"tile": "sand", "colour": "#efe2bf"}, "p": {"tile": "sand", "colour": "#ebb4b0"}, "t": {"tile": "tree_oak", "prop": "palm"}, "e": {"tile": "tree_pine", "colour": "#3d6a3a"}, "r": {"tile": "rock", "colour": "#dad2ba"}, "#": {"tile": "path", "colour": "#e6d8b8"}, "=": {"tile": "road", "colour": "#cbbfa6"}, ":": {"tile": "cobbles", "colour": "#d6c9ac"}, "f": {"tile": "flowers", "colour": "#78ad48"}}, "rows": ["$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$", "$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$", "$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$", "$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$", "$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$", "$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$", "$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$", "$$$$$$$$$$$$$w$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$", "$$$$$$$$wwwwwpwwwww$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$", "$$$$$$wwppppptpppspww$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$", "$$$$$wpp..........,ppw$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$", "$$$$wpt.BBBBBBBBB.,,,pw$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$", "$$$wp.f.BBBBBBBBB.,,.tpww$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$w$$$$$$$$$$$$$$", "$$wp....BBBBBBBBB..f.t.ppwwww$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$wwwwwpwwwww$$$$$$$$$", "$wst.:::BBBBDBBBB::::t.e.ppppww$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$ww